WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.334
Least privilege means people, services, workloads,

2
00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:08.519
and automation receive only the secret access they need to perform an approved job.

3
00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:15.113
A developer who supports one service does not automatically need every production database credential.

4
00:00:15.113 --> 00:00:21.336
A build job that deploys one application does not need administrator access to every cloud account.

5
00:00:21.336 --> 00:00:26.299
A monitoring tool may need read-only telemetry access, not broad write access.

6
00:00:26.299 --> 00:00:30.151
The principle is simple, but it takes discipline to maintain.

7
00:00:30.151 --> 00:00:34.522
Human access and machine access are different, but both need control.

8
00:00:34.522 --> 00:00:39.337
Human access should be tied to roles, teams, approvals, and periodic review.

9
00:00:39.337 --> 00:00:45.634
Machine access should be tied to service accounts, workload identities, deployment environments,

10
00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:47.412
and explicit ownership.

11
00:00:47.412 --> 00:00:54.672
A service account should not be a nameless shared identity that survives forever because everyone is afraid to touch

12
00:00:54.672 --> 00:00:56.450
it.

13
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.525
It should have an owner, a purpose, a scope, and a review schedule.

14
00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:06.674
Broad access to a secret manager can be as risky as broad access to the systems those secrets unlock.

15
00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:13.860
If an identity can read every production secret, that identity effectively has access to many production systems.

16
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:21.342
Vault administrator roles, break-glass accounts, synchronization jobs, and automation tokens need careful review.

17
00:01:21.342 --> 00:01:26.602
Administrative convenience can quietly become a single point of broad compromise.

18
00:01:26.602 --> 00:01:30.009
Short-lived credentials reduce long-term exposure.

19
00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:36.751
Instead of distributing permanent keys, teams can use managed identities, workload identity federation,

20
00:01:36.751 --> 00:01:41.937
just-in-time access, temporary tokens, or certificates with defined lifetimes.

21
00:01:41.937 --> 00:01:45.493
These patterns do not remove the need for access control,

22
00:01:45.493 --> 00:01:52.086
but they reduce the value of an old copied credential and make recovery easier when exposure is suspected.

23
00:01:52.086 --> 00:01:57.938
Break-glass access is sometimes necessary, but it should be rare, auditable, and practiced.

24
00:01:57.938 --> 00:02:04.383
Emergency access should have clear conditions, approval or notification expectations, monitoring,

25
00:02:04.383 --> 00:02:06.161
and after-action review.

26
00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:10.384
A break-glass path that is never tested may fail during an incident.

27
00:02:10.384 --> 00:02:15.570
A break-glass path that is too easy to use may become routine access by another name.

28
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.015
Access control is not a one-time setup.

29
00:02:18.015 --> 00:02:23.348
Teams change, services are replaced, pipelines evolve, and privileges accumulate.

30
00:02:23.348 --> 00:02:27.127
Regular access review is how a secrets program stays honest.

31
00:02:27.127 --> 00:02:32.535
Ask whether each identity still needs each secret, whether the scope is still correct,

32
00:02:32.535 --> 00:02:38.906
whether a shorter-lived or managed identity option exists, and whether audit logs show expected use.

33
00:02:38.906 --> 00:02:42.387
Least privilege is a maintenance habit, not a checkbox.
